WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our next guest is a talented
actor you know from films

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.939
like "The Hunger Games,"
"Logan Lucky," and "Scream."

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.174
He stars in the hit
Amazon series "The Boys."

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.677
New episodes stream Fridays
on Prime Video.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.877
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.613
-But I promised no more secrets,
right?

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.081
So --

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.485
-Oh, my God.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.253
Hughie.

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:22.589
-Hold on. One second.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:24.623
-You found more V.
-It's temporary V.

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:25.958
It's temporary, okay?

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:27.226
I'm not an idiot.

00:00:27.226 --> 00:00:28.494
I -- I know it's sketchy.

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:31.364
I've seen "Trainspotting."
But it's Soldier Boy.

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.566
I mean, what else
was I supposed to do?

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:36.736
-Please welcome to the show,
Jack Quaid, everybody.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.739
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:46.579 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:53.419 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.254
-Hey!
-Hey! Welcome to the show, Jack!

00:00:55.254 --> 00:00:57.022
I'm so happy to have you here.
-So good to be here.

00:00:57.022 --> 00:00:59.325
Thank you so much for having me.
-You're from LA.

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:01.493
You live there now, but you've
spent a lot of time in New York.

00:01:01.493 --> 00:01:04.797
-I did. I went to college here.
I went to NYU.

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.999
And as surreal as it is
to be here with you right now,

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:09.235
it's even more surreal to see
people like Henry Melcher

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:12.038
and Matt Reitman backstage.
-These are members of our staff,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.274
who we,
over the course of the day,

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:16.242
have been showing photos of
you guys in college together.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:20.112
-Yeah, yeah. Just doing comedy,
having acne, being dorks.

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.581
It's been great, yeah.
-You were living it up.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:22.949
You were ticking off
all the wish list.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.350
-The prime of my life.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.386
-Yes. Truly.
-Now "The Boys"

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.554
is a very dark show,
but it's also a very funny show.

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:30.290
-Yes.
-And I think those two

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:35.294
synthesized very well when
you were nominated for an Emmy,

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:36.529
but in the category
of Best Drama.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.664
-Yes, we were nominated --
We are a show

00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:40.933
in which people blow up.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.635
There's a lot of nudity.
There's a lot of blood.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.803
-Yep.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.705
-And, yeah, we were
nominated for Best Drama.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.174
And I think the way
that it happened was so funny.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:50.343
Like, you know,
there's that clip that they show

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.877
where they
actually nominate you.

00:01:51.877 --> 00:01:54.880
And it was a very serious man,
and he basically went,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.683
"And now the final category,
Best Drama."

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.619
And then it shows a clip of us,

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.921
and it's me screaming
in a speedboat

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.790
as we drive a speedboat into
the stomach of a beached whale.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.793
-Yeah.
-So that was the clip.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.128
And then they immediately cut
to "Bridgerton,"

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:12.298
which was just, like,
very serious period drama.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.534
And I was like, "This is, mwah.
This is chef's kiss."

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.836
-It really does,
I feel like, tell you --

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.304
let's just watch, because it
is exactly how you describe it.

00:02:19.304 --> 00:02:22.975
-I love this clip so much.
-Outstanding Drama Series.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.345
Here are the nominees.

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:27.714 line:0%
[ Men screaming ]
-"The Boys."

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.248 line:0%
[ Screaming, boat revving ]

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.117 line:0%
[ Squelch ]

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.885 line:0%
[ Whale shrieks ]

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.020 line:0%
-"Bridgerton."

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.156 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:40.593
-Now, it did cut away,

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.895
because the "Bridgerton" clip
also had a whale in it.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.063
-Oh, right.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.331
-That was that famous
"Bridgerton" scene.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.500
-It was a whale-heavy year.
A whale-heavy year.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.269
-They always say,
that was the whale year.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:51.770
-That was the whale year.
Definitely.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.740
-We saw from the clip,
your character, Hughie,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.309
did not originally
have superpowers.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.077
-No, no, yeah.
-And now you have the power

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.312
of teleportation,

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.180
and I feel like
one of the nice things about

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.282
the honest portrayal of
superheroes in "The Boys,"

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:06.385
is that you accept the fact
about teleportation

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:08.587
that a lot of
superhero shows would ignore.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:09.989
-Exactly, yeah.
So, you know,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:14.093
if you're teleporting, it's your
biological mass or whatever.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.463
I'm no scientist,
but it's your biological mass

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.765
going from one part of the room
to another,

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:23.002
so your clothes naturally
wouldn't come with you.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.671
So in our show,
whenever Hughie teleports,

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:28.007
his clothes
stay where he started,

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.609
and then I emerge
on the other side, naked.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.578
So I had a lot of nudity
this season.

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:34.347
Yeah.
-This is very exciting.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:35.748
This is what they offer you?

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:37.516
-Yeah, so this
is called modesty wear.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:40.853
And it may be worse
than being actually naked.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.187
So when you're shooting
the scene,

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:43.289
you're not actually naked.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.723
You're wearing one of these
things.

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:46.626
[ Audience groans ]

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:48.160
-And if you're wondering
how it goes on,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.363
they provide you with this.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:53.332
-It's like an at-home
COVID test, you know?

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:54.767
They have, like,
the instructions,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.536
and I think the worst one --
-How do you think...?

00:03:57.536 --> 00:03:59.538
Yeah, what do you think you do?
-That's the worst.

00:03:59.538 --> 00:04:01.974
-Oh, so this is like --
this is like, not -- yeah.

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:03.910
I get it now.
What did you choose?

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.244
-I don't know how
into detail I can get,

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:08.514
but I did not choose that one.
-So obviously, I mean,

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.016
the instructions
are too complicated.

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:13.585
-Yeah, no.
It's too highbrow for me.

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:14.887
For sure, definitely.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:18.358
-You were also
in the "Scream" film

00:04:18.358 --> 00:04:19.625
that came out.
-Yes.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:21.361
-That must've been
a fun project to be in.

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:23.462
And I feel like, I will say,

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:25.431
I feel like enough time
has passed

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.467
to say that you had
a very interesting role.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.804
-I did. And this is very weird
for me to say out loud.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.873
And audience,
please cover your ears

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.741
if you don't want "Scream"
spoiled for you.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:37.944
But I was the killer.

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.879
-Congratulations.
-Yeah.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.246
[ Cheers ]
-I mean, that's a big get.

00:04:41.246 --> 00:04:42.481
-That's a funny thing to
congratulate a person for.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:44.016
"You murdered!
Good for you."

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:45.952
-But the killer, to be
the killer in "Scream."

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.686
I mean, if you're
going to be a killer.

00:04:47.686 --> 00:04:48.955
-Yeah, it was awesome.

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.690
They told me initially,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.958
and then I immediately
didn't believe them,

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.927
because what they do when
they shoot a "Scream" movie is

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:57.664
they want to prevent leaks,
so they gave the entire cast

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:59.798
fake scripts
with different victims,

00:04:59.798 --> 00:05:02.000
different killers,
different everything.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.569
So they told me
I was the killer.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.037
I was like, "Okay."

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:08.607
And then we were shooting
in North Carolina and I had to,

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.943
like, keep this secret
from the rest of the cast

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:12.077
that I was the killer.
But then I thought --

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:13.046
-That's -- A lot of killers
have to do that.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.713
-Yes, they do.
-Yeah.

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:17.683
[ Laughter ]
-I was really in character.

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.753
Also on top of that,
we were playing Mafia a lot

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.187
when we were, like,
in our downtime.

00:05:22.187 --> 00:05:24.823
So we were playing a whodunit
while shooting a whodunit.

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:28.794
It was very meta.
But, yeah, it was really weird.

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:31.397
-Do you think there was some
intra-cast jealousy

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:32.898
when they found out
you were the actual killer?

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:34.366
-I don't know.

00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:36.135
I don't know if that's, like,
a thing you get jealous about,

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.870
necessarily,
but it was crazy,

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.805
because at one point,
I thought, "Wait a minute.

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.208
They said I'm the killer.
Maybe they're lying to me."

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:43.376
-Right.
-Maybe they're pulling, like,

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:45.945
a crazy other twist
and I'm gonna wind up,

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:49.148
like, dead in an alleyway
and not be a killer at all.

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.584
And that would have been,
I guess, bad?

00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:53.786
I don't know, yeah.

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.087
-Well, I can't believe
I'm saying it,

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.322
but congrats
on being the killer.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:58.591
-Thanks.
I guess I prefer to murder.

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.092
That's great.
-Well, I'm glad.

00:06:00.092 --> 00:06:01.694
You know, we try to dig in
on this show

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.162
and get to the bottom
of who our guests are,

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.329
and I'm glad we found that out
about you.

00:06:04.329 --> 00:06:05.898
-I am a serial killer.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.032
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:08.200
Hello, America.
[ Applause ]

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.569
-Hey, man, congrats again
on the show.

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:13.005
It's so special and so unique,
and you're so wonderful on it.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.607
Thank you for being here.
You guys, Jack Quaid.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:18.944
New episodes of "The Boys"
stream Fridays on Prime Video.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.879
We'll be right back
with Sandy Honig,

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:23.216
Mitra Jouhari,
and Alyssa Stonoha.

